# UPGRADE-07
# Distributed Engineering Orchestrator, GitHub PR Automation,
# CI Feedback Loop & Autonomous Repository Operations

You are working directly inside ZCoder after Upgrade-06.

Expected current baseline:

- v1.40.0 remains the working unreleased baseline unless repository state proves otherwise
- durable SQLite JobStore exists
- structured EventStore exists
- approval persistence exists
- DirectRuntime exists
- AgentSDKRuntime exists
- ManagedAgentRuntime exists
- FakeRuntime exists
- policy/validation infrastructure exists
- package build/release gates exist
- Anthropic conformance infrastructure exists
- current test suite reports approximately:
  535 passed
  2 optional skips
  0 failures

Do NOT trust these claims merely because Upgrade-06 reported them.

================================================================
MISSION
================================================================

Upgrade ZCoder from a single-machine autonomous job runtime into a
production-grade engineering orchestration platform capable of:

1. receiving engineering tasks
2. scheduling them safely
3. creating isolated Git workspaces
4. executing agents through supported runtimes
5. producing reviewed commits
6. opening GitHub pull requests
7. observing GitHub CI/check results
8. diagnosing CI failures
9. performing bounded repair loops
10. updating the same PR safely
11. handling human review/approval
12. using protected branches and merge queues correctly
13. merging only when repository policy permits
14. preserving a durable audit trail
15. recovering after process crashes
16. avoiding duplicate work
17. controlling concurrency and spend
18. coordinating multiple repositories/jobs where appropriate

Target architecture:

Task
  │
  ▼
ZCoder Orchestrator
  │
  ├── Scheduler
  ├── Job Store
  ├── Runtime Router
  ├── Policy / Approval Engine
  ├── Git Workspace Manager
  ├── GitHub Adapter
  ├── CI Observer
  ├── Repair Controller
  ├── Review Controller
  └── Merge Controller
        │
        ▼
GitHub
  ├── Branch
  ├── Pull Request
  ├── Checks
  ├── Reviews
  ├── Actions
  ├── Rulesets
  └── Merge Queue

Do NOT turn ZCoder into an uncontrolled auto-merge bot.

================================================================
PHASE 0 — FORENSIC VERIFICATION OF UPGRADE-06
================================================================

Before building distributed functionality, prove that Upgrade-06's
runtime architecture is real.

Run:

git status --short
git log -20 --oneline
git tag --sort=-version:refname | head -20

python -m pytest -q
python main.py --help
python main.py --version
python -m build
git diff --check

Inspect:

agent_runtime.py
all runtime adapter classes
job persistence
event persistence
approval persistence
policy engine
validation pipeline
tests/test_agent_runtime.py
all related CLI wiring

Produce:

UPGRADE-06 RUNTIME VERIFICATION

Capability | Claimed | Actual | Evidence | Test Coverage | Status

Statuses:

VERIFIED
PARTIAL
DEAD_WIRING
MOCK_ONLY
UNTESTED
INCORRECT
MISSING

Do not proceed with distributed scheduling until all P0 runtime defects
are fixed.

================================================================
1. RUNTIME CONTRACT TEST EXPANSION
================================================================

Six runtime tests are not sufficient for production claims.

Build comprehensive contract tests for every runtime implementation.

Every adapter must be tested for:

start
status
events
cancel
resume
failure
timeout
approval
budget reached
reconnection
event deduplication
cleanup

Use shared tests where semantics are truly common.

Do NOT force unsupported capabilities to pass fake tests.

Represent unsupported behavior explicitly.

Example:

RuntimeCapabilities(
    resume=True,
    remote_reconnect=False,
    multiagent=False,
    ...
)

================================================================
2. AGENT SDK RUNTIME — REALITY CHECK
================================================================

Verify AgentSDKRuntime against the CURRENT official Claude Agent SDK.

Test real adapter wiring separately from FakeRuntime.

Trace:

ZCoder job
 -> AgentSDKRuntime.start()
 -> actual Agent SDK call
 -> session identifier
 -> event stream
 -> ZCoder normalized events
 -> completion state

Verify:

sessions
resume
permissions
hooks
subagents
MCP
skills
cancellation
errors

Do not count a wrapper that never invokes the SDK as IMPLEMENTED.

Use mocks at the SDK boundary for offline CI.

Optional live tests may exist separately.

================================================================
3. MANAGED AGENTS RUNTIME — REALITY CHECK
================================================================

Re-fetch current official Managed Agents documentation.

Verify actual:

agent creation/reference
environment
session creation
session events
thread events
budgets
resume
cancel/stop semantics
multiagent roster
memory
remote identifiers

Trace actual HTTP/SDK wire shape independently.

Test:

start session
persist session ID
consume events
disconnect
reconnect
deduplicate
budget_reached
budget update
resume
terminal state
cancel

Do not allow local JobStore state to masquerade as remote resumability.

================================================================
4. STATE MACHINE EXPANSION
================================================================

Audit current job state transitions exhaustively.

Generate transition tests for every pair.

Expected examples:

CREATED -> PLANNING
PLANNING -> READY
READY -> RUNNING
RUNNING -> WAITING_APPROVAL
WAITING_APPROVAL -> RUNNING
RUNNING -> BUDGET_REACHED
BUDGET_REACHED -> RUNNING
RUNNING -> FAILED
RUNNING -> SUCCEEDED
RUNNING -> CANCELLED

Reject illegal transitions.

Use a central transition table.

Do not scatter state rules across handlers.

================================================================
5. CRASH RECOVERY TEST MATRIX
================================================================

Simulate process death during:

PLANNING
RUNNING
WAITING_APPROVAL
VALIDATING
RETRYING
BUDGET_REACHED

Then rebuild service objects from SQLite.

Verify jobs become:

reconciled
resumable
failed
or operator-action-required

according to explicit policy.

Never leave stale RUNNING records forever.

================================================================
6. RACE CONDITION TESTS
================================================================

Test:

approval and cancel arriving simultaneously
cancel and tool completion simultaneously
budget reached while cancellation happens
remote completion during reconnect
duplicate webhook/events
two workers claiming same job
retry while previous attempt finishes

Use synchronization primitives, not arbitrary sleep().

================================================================
7. DISTRIBUTED WORKER MODEL
================================================================

Introduce a worker abstraction.

Concept:

Worker:
    id
    hostname
    capabilities
    runtimes
    max_concurrency
    heartbeat
    last_seen
    status

Worker status:

ONLINE
DRAINING
OFFLINE
DEGRADED

Jobs should be claimable by workers.

Do not immediately introduce Kubernetes.

Support local multi-worker processes first.

================================================================
8. DURABLE JOB CLAIMING
================================================================

Prevent two workers from executing the same job.

Implement atomic job claiming using SQLite transaction semantics for the
local backend.

Fields may include:

claimed_by
claimed_at
lease_expires_at

Claiming must be atomic.

Test concurrent claim attempts.

Exactly one worker wins.

================================================================
9. LEASES
================================================================

Use leases so a crashed worker does not permanently own a job.

Support:

lease acquire
lease heartbeat
lease renewal
lease expiration
reclaim

Protect against duplicate execution.

Take particular care when a Managed Agent remote session may still be
running after the local worker lease expires.

Reconciliation must occur before launching a replacement execution.

================================================================
10. WORKER HEARTBEATS
================================================================

Workers should periodically record liveness.

Do not write heartbeats so frequently that SQLite becomes a bottleneck.

Make interval configurable.

Scheduler should avoid OFFLINE workers.

================================================================
11. SCHEDULER
================================================================

Build a scheduler with bounded concurrency.

Inputs:

job priority
runtime requirement
repository
worker capability
budget
workspace availability
operator policy

Initial priorities:

LOW
NORMAL
HIGH

Do not allow starvation.

Implement deterministic selection where possible.

================================================================
12. QUEUE STATES
================================================================

Extend job lifecycle if necessary:

QUEUED
CLAIMED

Do so carefully and migrate existing databases safely.

Example:

CREATED
 -> PLANNING
 -> READY
 -> QUEUED
 -> CLAIMED
 -> RUNNING

Do not create state explosion unless semantics require it.

================================================================
13. PER-REPOSITORY CONCURRENCY
================================================================

Concurrent jobs touching the same repository need controls.

Policies:

parallel-worktrees
serialize
read-only-parallel
exclusive

Default mutation jobs should use independent worktrees.

Configure a maximum concurrent mutation count per repository.

================================================================
14. RESOURCE CONCURRENCY
================================================================

Support limits such as:

max_total_jobs
max_local_jobs
max_managed_jobs
max_agent_sdk_jobs
max_validators
max_git_operations

Avoid unbounded asyncio.gather().

================================================================
15. FAIRNESS
================================================================

Prevent high-volume repositories from starving others.

Consider simple weighted/fair scheduling.

Do not build an unnecessarily complex cluster scheduler.

================================================================
16. GITHUB ADAPTER
================================================================

Create a dedicated GitHub integration boundary.

Do NOT scatter GitHub HTTP calls through orchestration code.

Concept:

GitHubProvider:
    get_repository()
    create_branch()
    push_branch()
    create_pull_request()
    get_pull_request()
    list_reviews()
    list_checks()
    get_workflow_runs()
    add_comment()
    update_pull_request()
    request_review()
    merge_pull_request()
    enqueue_merge()

Use an official/current GitHub API surface.

If GitHub CLI is retained as an adapter, isolate it.

================================================================
17. AUTHENTICATION
================================================================

Support appropriate GitHub auth types:

GitHub App
fine-grained PAT where suitable
GITHUB_TOKEN in Actions contexts where sufficient

Prefer GitHub App authentication for autonomous service operation.

Never log tokens.

Never store plaintext tokens in JobStore/EventStore.

Represent:

CredentialRef

not secret value.

================================================================
18. GITHUB PERMISSION MINIMIZATION
================================================================

Determine minimum required permissions.

Examples may include:

contents
pull_requests
checks/actions read
issues/comments
metadata

Do NOT request administration permissions merely for convenience.

Merge/ruleset administration should remain separate.

Document exact permission requirements.

================================================================
19. REPOSITORY REGISTRATION
================================================================

Create repository configuration.

Example:

.zcoder/config.yaml

repository:
  default_branch: main

github:
  owner: ...
  repo: ...

automation:
  create_pr: true
  auto_merge: false

policy:
  required_validators:
    - tests

Do not require GitHub for local-only usage.

================================================================
20. GIT WORKFLOW
================================================================

A GitHub-backed job should use:

base SHA
isolated branch
isolated worktree
commits
push
PR

Record:

repository
base_ref
base_sha
branch
head_sha
PR number
PR URL/id

Never depend only on branch names for identity.

================================================================
21. BRANCH NAMING
================================================================

Generate safe deterministic-ish branches:

zcoder/job-<short-id>-<slug>

Validate Git refs.

Avoid collisions.

Do not allow user task text to inject arbitrary refs.

================================================================
22. COMMIT POLICY
================================================================

Agents may edit files, but commits should be created through the
orchestrator.

Record:

commit SHA
author policy
message
job ID

Suggested trailer:

ZCoder-Job: job_...

Do not forge human authorship.

Use an identifiable bot/service identity where appropriate.

================================================================
23. COMMIT CONTENT VERIFICATION
================================================================

Before commit:

inspect actual git diff.

Reject:

unexpected files
secret files
credential material
oversized binaries
paths outside allowed scope

Run configured validators.

================================================================
24. PUSH POLICY
================================================================

Pushing is an external mutation.

Policy engine must classify it separately from local git commit.

Possible policy:

ALLOW for job-owned branch
REQUIRE_APPROVAL for arbitrary branch
DENY for protected default branch

Never force-push default/protected branches.

================================================================
25. PULL REQUEST CREATION
================================================================

Add support for creating PRs.

A PR should contain:

summary
changes
validation
risk
job ID
runtime
test results
known limitations

Do not fill PR body with raw internal chain-of-thought.

Use concise execution evidence.

================================================================
26. PR IDEMPOTENCY
================================================================

Retrying the same job must not create duplicate PRs.

Persist PR number/node ID.

Before creating a PR, check whether the job already owns one.

================================================================
27. PR UPDATE LOOP
================================================================

If CI fails:

same job
same branch
same PR

unless policy explicitly requires a new attempt/PR.

Do not create PR spam.

================================================================
28. CI OBSERVER
================================================================

Implement GitHub CI/check observation.

Normalize:

PENDING
RUNNING
SUCCESS
FAILURE
CANCELLED
TIMED_OUT
ACTION_REQUIRED
SKIPPED
NEUTRAL

Capture:

check name
workflow
run ID
URL/reference
conclusion
started/completed times

Do not scrape rendered GitHub pages.

================================================================
29. REQUIRED CHECKS
================================================================

Differentiate:

all observed checks

from:

required checks

Repository protection/ruleset semantics should remain authoritative.

Do not allow ZCoder to declare a PR mergeable merely because one test
workflow passed.

================================================================
30. CI FAILURE COLLECTION
================================================================

When CI fails:

collect enough logs/artifacts to diagnose the failure.

Do not blindly feed megabytes of raw logs into an LLM.

Pipeline:

download
 -> normalize
 -> locate failing steps
 -> extract bounded relevant context
 -> save full log as artifact
 -> give agent concise failure context

================================================================
31. LOG SECURITY
================================================================

GitHub Actions logs may contain secrets or sensitive content.

Redact known credentials before presenting logs to agents/events.

Respect GitHub secret masking but do not assume it catches everything.

================================================================
32. CI REPAIR LOOP
================================================================

Implement:

PR opened
 -> checks running
 -> checks fail
 -> diagnose
 -> repair
 -> validate locally
 -> commit
 -> push
 -> checks rerun

Bound repair attempts.

Example:

max_ci_repairs = 3

After limit:

WAITING_OPERATOR
or FAILED_VALIDATION

No infinite repair loops.

================================================================
33. FAILURE FINGERPRINTING
================================================================

Fingerprint CI failures using structured information.

Examples:

workflow
job
step
test
error class

If the identical failure repeats after multiple repair attempts,
escalate instead of looping.

================================================================
34. STALE BASE HANDLING
================================================================

Default branch may advance while agent works.

Detect base drift before merge.

Policies:

rebase
merge-base-update
re-run
operator-required

Never silently rewrite complicated conflicts.

================================================================
35. REBASE SAFETY
================================================================

Automated rebase is allowed only for the agent-owned branch and subject
to policy.

If conflicts occur:

agent may attempt conflict resolution within bounded workflow

but must re-run validators.

Never rewrite another contributor's branch.

================================================================
36. HUMAN REVIEW
================================================================

Observe PR reviews.

Normalize:

APPROVED
CHANGES_REQUESTED
COMMENTED
DISMISSED

Human review takes precedence over autonomous merge desire.

Do not automatically dismiss reviews.

================================================================
37. REVIEW FEEDBACK INGESTION
================================================================

When a reviewer requests changes:

convert review comments into structured work items.

Preserve:

author
file
line/context
comment ID
timestamp

Do not treat every discussion comment as mandatory instruction.

Policy may distinguish trusted reviewers.

================================================================
38. REVIEW REPAIR LOOP
================================================================

Workflow:

CHANGES_REQUESTED
 -> analyze feedback
 -> plan modifications
 -> edit
 -> validate
 -> commit
 -> push
 -> reply/update status

Bound attempts.

Do not mark human feedback resolved without actual evidence.

================================================================
39. AUTOMATED REVIEW AGENT
================================================================

Optionally run an independent ZCoder reviewer before PR or merge.

Reviewer should receive:

diff
relevant architecture
test results

not implementation agent's hidden reasoning.

Review:

correctness
security
tests
compatibility
maintainability

================================================================
40. CHECK RUN / STATUS REPORTING
================================================================

Where appropriate, publish ZCoder job status back to GitHub.

Example:

ZCoder / Agent Validation

States should reflect actual job status.

Do not publish SUCCESS while required validators remain pending.

================================================================
41. COMMENTS
================================================================

ZCoder may post concise status comments.

Avoid noisy per-event comments.

Prefer:

start
meaningful blocker
final summary

or Check Runs where better.

================================================================
42. MERGE POLICY
================================================================

Merge must be governed by explicit policy.

Example:

merge:
  enabled: true
  require:
    - zcoder_validators
    - github_required_checks
    - required_reviews
  strategy: squash
  queue: preferred

Default should NOT automatically merge arbitrary repositories.

================================================================
43. PROTECTED BRANCHES / RULESETS
================================================================

Do not bypass protected branch or ruleset requirements.

Never use administrator override to make automation easier.

If repository policy blocks merge:

report WAITING_POLICY

not ERROR.

================================================================
44. MERGE QUEUE
================================================================

Support GitHub merge queue only when repository configuration supports it.

Important:

GitHub Actions workflows used as required checks for merge queue must
handle the merge_group event.

Audit repository workflows before enabling queue automation.

Do not assume pull_request-only workflows will run correctly in a merge
queue.

================================================================
45. MERGE QUEUE AUTH
================================================================

Verify CURRENT GitHub authentication requirements before implementing
programmatic enqueue behavior.

Do not assume GITHUB_TOKEN has all queue permissions.

Support GitHub App credentials when necessary.

Fail with a clear policy/authentication error rather than bypassing the
queue.

================================================================
46. AUTO-MERGE
================================================================

Distinguish:

GitHub auto-merge
merge queue
direct merge

These are separate mechanisms.

ZCoder should select based on repository policy.

Do not implement direct merge fallback if queue is mandatory.

================================================================
47. MERGE STATE MACHINE
================================================================

Introduce explicit PR automation states as necessary:

NO_PR
PR_OPEN
CI_PENDING
CI_FAILED
REVIEW_PENDING
CHANGES_REQUESTED
MERGE_READY
QUEUED_FOR_MERGE
MERGED
CLOSED

Do not overload Job.status with every GitHub-specific detail.

Use a separate repository/PR state model.

================================================================
48. POST-MERGE VERIFICATION
================================================================

After merge:

verify resulting commit is reachable from expected default branch.

Record:

merge SHA
merged_at
merge method

Optionally observe post-merge CI.

Do not claim job fully delivered if merge API response was ambiguous.

================================================================
49. ISSUE-TO-JOB
================================================================

Optionally support creating a job from a GitHub issue.

Example:

zcoder github run-issue 123

Fetch:

title
body
labels
repository metadata

Treat issue content as UNTRUSTED user input.

Do not obey embedded instructions that override security/policy.

================================================================
50. PR-TO-JOB
================================================================

Support jobs such as:

zcoder github review-pr 456
zcoder github fix-pr 456

Never mutate someone else's branch unless repository policy explicitly
permits it.

Preferred fix strategy may be separate agent-owned branch/PR.

================================================================
51. WEBHOOK RECEIVER
================================================================

Support GitHub webhooks where useful:

pull_request
pull_request_review
check_run
check_suite
workflow_run
push
issues
issue_comment

Do not enable every event unnecessarily.

Verify signatures using GitHub's current documented mechanism.

Reject invalid payloads.

================================================================
52. WEBHOOK IDEMPOTENCY
================================================================

Persist delivery IDs.

Duplicate delivery must not:

start duplicate job
post duplicate comment
merge twice
rerun repair twice

================================================================
53. WEBHOOK REPLAY DEFENSE
================================================================

Verify signature and delivery identity.

Apply bounded retention for processed delivery IDs.

Do not trust user-controlled headers.

================================================================
54. POLLING FALLBACK
================================================================

GitHub integrations must remain usable without public webhook ingress.

Support polling/status refresh as a fallback.

Avoid aggressive polling.

Use backoff.

================================================================
55. EVENT NORMALIZATION
================================================================

Normalize GitHub events into the shared ZCoder event system.

Examples:

github.pr.created
github.check.started
github.check.failed
github.review.approved
github.review.changes_requested
github.merge.queued
github.pr.merged

Keep raw payload reference separately where necessary.

================================================================
56. EVENT DEDUPLICATION
================================================================

Same GitHub state may arrive via:

webhook
polling
manual refresh

Deduplicate.

Side effects must be idempotent.

================================================================
57. CROSS-SYSTEM CORRELATION
================================================================

Record durable mappings:

ZCoder job ID
Git repository
branch
commit
GitHub PR
GitHub workflow run
Anthropic session
runtime thread

This should make one job traceable end-to-end.

================================================================
58. DISTRIBUTED EVENT ORDERING
================================================================

Do not assume GitHub and runtime events arrive chronologically.

Use:

source timestamp
ingest timestamp
local sequence

State reducers must tolerate late events.

================================================================
59. OUTBOX PATTERN
================================================================

For local persistent mutations that require an external GitHub side
effect, consider a durable outbox.

Example:

DB transaction:
  update job state
  record event
  enqueue "github.create_pr"

Worker:
  process outbox
  mark delivered

This prevents crashes between state update and remote call.

Do not introduce external message brokers yet unless necessary.

================================================================
60. IDEMPOTENCY KEYS FOR EXTERNAL ACTIONS
================================================================

For each external mutation define idempotency behavior:

push
PR creation
comment
review request
merge
queue enrollment

Where GitHub API lacks native idempotency keys, use local durable
correlation and reconciliation.

================================================================
61. DISTRIBUTED LOCKING
================================================================

For SQLite/local deployment use database transactional locking/leases.

Do not fake distributed safety with Python threading.Lock.

If multiple hosts truly share no transactional datastore, report that
SQLite backend supports only single-host/multi-process orchestration.

Design interface for future Postgres.

================================================================
62. STORAGE ABSTRACTION
================================================================

Evolve persistence behind interfaces:

JobStore
EventStore
ApprovalStore
WorkerStore
OutboxStore
RepositoryStore

Do not immediately implement every backend.

SQLite remains default.

Prepare PostgreSQL adapter boundary.

================================================================
63. DATABASE MIGRATIONS
================================================================

Upgrade-07 schema changes require explicit migration.

Track schema version.

Migration must preserve Upgrade-06 jobs/events.

Test migration from previous schema.

================================================================
64. SCHEDULER RECOVERY
================================================================

Restart scheduler with:

queued jobs
claimed expired jobs
managed remote sessions
open GitHub PRs
pending CI

Reconcile all without duplicating work.

================================================================
65. REPOSITORY RECONCILIATION
================================================================

A branch or PR may change while ZCoder is offline.

On recovery re-fetch:

branch SHA
PR state
reviews
checks
merge state

Do not trust stale local GitHub state.

================================================================
66. MANAGED AGENT RECONCILIATION
================================================================

If a job used Managed Agents and its local worker died:

check remote session first.

If remote session is still running:

reconnect

not start another session.

================================================================
67. OPERATOR CONTROLS
================================================================

Add coherent commands:

zcoder workers list
zcoder workers show ID
zcoder workers drain ID

zcoder queue list

zcoder github repos
zcoder github status JOB

zcoder jobs show JOB
zcoder jobs watch JOB
zcoder jobs retry JOB
zcoder jobs cancel JOB

Exact command structure may follow existing CLI style.

================================================================
68. GITHUB JOB SUBMISSION
================================================================

Target workflow:

zcoder run \
  "Fix failing authentication tests" \
  --repo OWNER/REPO \
  --github-pr

Expected behavior:

clone/worktree
branch
agent work
validation
commit
push
PR
CI observation

No auto-merge unless explicitly configured.

================================================================
69. DRY RUN
================================================================

Support:

--dry-run

Show intended:

runtime
workspace
branch
tools
GitHub mutations
validators
approval requirements
merge policy

Dry-run must not push/create PR/merge.

================================================================
70. NON-INTERACTIVE AUTOMATION
================================================================

For CI/service mode:

--non-interactive

Approval-required action should transition to:

WAITING_APPROVAL

not hang on stdin.

================================================================
71. POLICY PRECEDENCE
================================================================

Define policy precedence clearly.

Example:

global
 -> repository
 -> job

But lower-level policy must not be able to weaken immutable global
security restrictions unless explicit override mechanism exists.

================================================================
72. TRUSTED REPOSITORIES
================================================================

Do not treat all repositories equally.

Configuration may classify:

trusted
restricted
untrusted

Untrusted repositories should default to stricter network/tool/secret
policy.

================================================================
73. FORK PR SECURITY
================================================================

Pull requests from forks are a major trust boundary.

Never expose write credentials/secrets to untrusted fork code.

Do not run privileged autonomous mutation workflow on fork-controlled
code without explicit safeguards.

================================================================
74. ACTIONS SECURITY
================================================================

Audit generated/modified GitHub Actions workflows carefully.

Prevent agents from introducing workflow changes that exfiltrate
secrets.

Treat modifications under:

.github/workflows/

as high-risk.

Require additional approval or security validation.

================================================================
75. CODEOWNERS / SENSITIVE PATHS
================================================================

Optionally define sensitive paths:

.github/workflows/**
security/**
infra/**
terraform/**
deployment/**
authentication/**

Changes to sensitive paths may require:

human review
special reviewer
stronger validator
no auto-merge

================================================================
76. SECRET DETECTION
================================================================

Scan diff before:

commit
push
PR

Block probable credentials.

False positives should produce actionable approval/error flow.

Never upload suspected secret contents into model prompts to explain
them.

================================================================
77. DEPENDENCY CHANGES
================================================================

Detect dependency manifest/lock changes.

Require appropriate:

tests
security checks
lock consistency

Avoid automatic dependency upgrade unrelated to task.

================================================================
78. BINARY FILE POLICY
================================================================

Autonomous jobs should not unexpectedly add large binaries.

Set configurable thresholds.

Require approval when exceeded.

================================================================
79. COST BUDGET ACROSS REPAIR LOOPS
================================================================

One GitHub engineering task may involve several agent runs.

Budget should apply to whole job, not reset silently on each CI repair.

Track:

initial implementation
review
CI repairs
human-feedback repairs

================================================================
80. RUNTIME SELECTION FOR REPAIR
================================================================

The runtime used for initial implementation does not have to be the
runtime used for a small repair.

However, runtime switching must be explicit/auditable.

Record:

runtime_per_attempt

================================================================
81. MULTIAGENT ENGINEERING STRATEGY
================================================================

Optional workflow:

planner
implementer
tester
reviewer

Use parallelism only for independent work.

Do not let two agents modify overlapping files concurrently without
merge/conflict strategy.

================================================================
82. REPOSITORY INDEXING
================================================================

For larger repositories create lightweight repo metadata:

languages
test commands
build commands
package managers
important paths

Persist reusable metadata carefully.

Invalidate when relevant files change.

Do not create a giant vector system unless justified.

================================================================
83. PROJECT INSTRUCTIONS
================================================================

Respect:

AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md where runtime legitimately uses it
repository docs
ZCoder project policy

Security policy remains higher priority.

================================================================
84. VALIDATION DISCOVERY
================================================================

Do not blindly execute every script in repository.

Discover likely validators, then pass through tool policy.

Prefer explicit repository configuration.

================================================================
85. GITHUB ACTIONS CONCURRENCY
================================================================

When ZCoder adds or modifies Actions workflows, understand GitHub
concurrency semantics.

Use concurrency groups where duplicate workflow execution would be
harmful.

Do not cancel active deployment-like workflows merely because a newer
PR update exists unless policy explicitly calls for it.

================================================================
86. MERGE_GROUP EVENT
================================================================

When repository uses merge queue, verify required Actions workflows
include appropriate:

on:
  pull_request:
  merge_group:

or repository-appropriate equivalent.

Do not automatically patch workflows unless task/policy permits it.

Report misconfiguration clearly.

================================================================
87. CHECK CONCLUSION SEMANTICS
================================================================

Do not treat all non-failure conclusions as SUCCESS.

Handle:

success
failure
cancelled
timed_out
action_required
neutral
skipped
stale

according to repository rules and GitHub semantics.

================================================================
88. MERGE READINESS EXPLAINER
================================================================

Add:

zcoder github merge-status JOB

Example:

NOT_READY

- CI: PASS
- ZCoder validation: PASS
- Reviews: 0/1
- Merge queue: required
- Security approval: pending

